  "notes": "Java names a dependency by package, and the package is a directory: `import com.acme.app.model.Circle` resolves to `com/acme/app/model/Circle.java`, a wildcard import resolves to every file of the directory, and a *same-package* reference needs no statement at all — `sealed interface Shape permits Circle` is a dependency edge with nothing to parse an import out of. Those edges are annotated here (tag `same_package`); whether a parser can find them by scanning type references is the measured question, and the FP risk is that a type-shaped identifier that is not a corpus type produces an edge that is not there. Records generate accessors for their components, and generated members are annotated as definitions, exactly as `@dataclass` members are in Python. Constructors are not symbols; neither are fields, so no Java file has a `const` row. `module-info.java` declares the module and its `exports` list, which is the authority for API surface (`e3.7`) though not for nameability inside this module. Export is the module's external surface: a `public` top-level type in a package that `module-info.java` exports. A package-private type (tag `package_private`) is nameable only inside its own package, and a type in a package the module does not export (`com.acme.app.legacy`) is nameable inside this module but not outside it, so neither is an export — which is why `module-info.java` has to be read, not skipped.",
